David Damerell wrote:
> Quoting  Link <chillynois@yahoo.com>:
> >This is the part I disagree with.  If someone is recklessly doing melee
> >combat, we shouldn't encourage them to use a heavily ranged strategy.
> >Instead, we should teach them not to be reckless.

> Which means ranged attacks, if you aren't going to be in denial about
> their high damage and low risk of reprisals.

Which means evaluating each situation, and using the most
appropriate response.

> >1) Retreated into a chokepoint so that he could melee the monsters
> >1-by-1.

> Why not retreat into a chokepoint and then shoot the monsters as they rush
> up to it, then melee the survivors?

Because one of the survivors might be the sort of monster
you definitly don't want to mel=E9e.  I'd mel=E9e the
run-of-the-mill monsters, in order to be sure that I had the
ranged attacks available later just in case.

But it obviously depends.  In one game, I was a ranger with
more than 700 +6 arrows before I'd finished the mines.  I
don't think I mel=E9e'd once after that.  Even over water; who
cares if I loose a few.  In another, as a Valk two-weaponing
a +7 Grayswandir and a +6 Katana, I went ahead and mel=E9e'd
monsters I'd normally avoid, like nymphs or mind
flayers---with that kind of mel=E9e, you're pretty much
guaranteed a kill with the first hit.  Most of the time,
however, the choice isn't so obvious.

> >2) Led one of the dangerous monsters into a trap, so that he didn't
> >have to melee all the monsters at the same time.

> Why not use ranged attacks on it while it is stuck and not melee it at
> all?

Because afterwards, you've got to step on the trap yourself,
to get your daggers or arrows back?  Because afterwards,
you'll be too low on manna to be able to continue spell
casting if something really dangerous comes along?

> >3) Somehow disable some of the dangerous monsters (i.e. mirror or
> >Elebereth) so that he didn't have to melee all the monsters at the same
> >time.

> Why not use ranged attacks on the undisabled monsters?

As above.  Also, for some roles, you'll have more hit points
than manna, and they'll regenerate faster (and a good AC can
mean that you'll loose a lot less of them than you'd loose
of manna.)

    [...]
> >Yes, it is sometimes better to just use ranged damage, but we shouldn't
> >be equating melee with recklessness, and ranged with thoughtfulness.

> We're not. We're implying they are closely correlated.

Which is wrong.  Throwing daggers to kill a gnome when
there's a nymph in the room is careless.  More generally,
not evaluating the state you'll be in after whatever you do
is careless.

> >We should be encouraging people to evaluate their options on a
> >case-by-case basis.

> And generally a) the right answer on a case-by-case basis is ranged
> attacks

For some characters.  For roles like Valkyrie or Samurai,
I'd guess that the best case-by-case answer is mel=E9e more
than 80% of the time.

> and b) the selected answer, for players who have not read Jove's
> illuminating analyses, is melee.

Her analysis certainly helped me to realize the potential of
ranged combat, and to add it to my repertoire.  But not to
use it exclusively---it depends on the character, and the
situation.

> >They aren't directly saying that, but their posts can be easily
> >misinterpreted to mean that.  When people explicitly say "throw daggers
> >early and often" over and over again, and NEVER explictily promote any
> >melee combat, people can misinterpret those posts as "ranged is the
> >answer to everything".

> People could misinterpret your posts as "rush up to Demogorgon with cries
> of glee", but we will extend you the courtesy of talking about what you
> actually write, not what someone _might_ misinterpret them to mean. Please
> do the same.

I'm sorry, but when I hear "throw daggers early and often",
it sounds very much to me as if throwing daggers should
always be the preferred method.

